# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. GENERAL PRINCIPLES
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## Code Quality
- Write clean, self-documenting code. Prefer readability over cleverness.
- Follow the DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) principle, but avoid premature abstraction.
- Every function/component should do ONE thing well (Single Responsibility Principle).
- Delete dead code immediately. Don't comment it out.
- Avoid magic numbers and strings — use named constants or enums.

## Naming Conventions
- Components: PascalCase (e.g., `UserProfileCard`)
- Functions/variables: camelCase (e.g., `fetchUserData`)
- Constants: SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE (e.g., `MAX_RETRY_COUNT`)
- Files: kebab-case for utilities (e.g., `api-client.ts`), PascalCase for components
- Custom hooks: prefix with `use` (e.g., `useAuthSession`)
- AI-related utilities: prefix with `ai` (e.g., `aiGenerateSummary`)

## TypeScript First
- Always use TypeScript. Never use `any` — use `unknown` and narrow the type.
- Define explicit return types on all public functions.
- Use `interface` for objects you'll extend; use `type` for unions and intersections.
- Use Zod or similar for runtime validation of all external/AI data.

## Comments
- Explain WHY, not WHAT. The code shows what; comments explain intent.
- Add JSDoc on all exported functions and hooks.
- Mark incomplete work with `// TODO:` and technical debt with `// FIXME:`.
- For AI prompts embedded in code, add a comment block explaining the prompt's purpose.


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 2. MOBILE DEVELOPMENT (React Native / Expo)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## Project Structure
```
src/
  app/               # Expo Router screens or navigation
  components/        # Reusable UI components
    common/          # Truly generic (Button, Input, Card)
    features/        # Feature-scoped components
  hooks/             # Custom React hooks
  services/          # API calls, device services
  store/             # State management (Zustand / Redux)
  ai/                # All AI/LLM logic isolated here
    prompts/         # Prompt templates
    hooks/           # AI-specific hooks
    utils/           # Token counting, context management
  constants/         # App-wide constants
  types/             # Shared TypeScript types
  utils/             # Pure helper functions
  assets/            # Images, fonts, icons
```

## Component Rules
- Every component file exports ONE default component.
- Co-locate component styles in the same file (StyleSheet.create).
- Never hardcode colors or spacing — use your design token system.
- All touchable elements MUST have `accessibilityLabel` and `accessibilityRole`.
- Minimum touch target size: 44×44 points (Apple/Google HIG requirement).
- Use `React.memo` on list-item components rendered in FlatList.

## Performance
- Use `FlatList` or `FlashList` for any list with more than 20 items. Never `ScrollView` + `map()` for long lists.
- Always provide `keyExtractor` to FlatList — never rely on index as key for dynamic lists.
- Avoid anonymous functions in JSX props that cause unnecessary re-renders.
  ```tsx
  // BAD
  <Button onPress={() => handlePress(item.id)} />
  // GOOD
  const handleItemPress = useCallback(() => handlePress(item.id), [item.id]);
  <Button onPress={handleItemPress} />
  ```
- Use `useMemo` for expensive computations, not as a default.
- Lazy-load heavy screens using `React.lazy` or dynamic imports in Expo Router.
- Avoid `useEffect` for derived state — compute it inline or with `useMemo`.

## Navigation (Expo Router / React Navigation)
- Use file-based routing with Expo Router whenever possible.
- Typed navigation params are mandatory — define a `RootParamList`.
- Deep link support must be considered from day one — define `linking` config early.
- Screen components should be thin — delegate logic to hooks/services.

## Platform Handling
- Use `Platform.select()` or `Platform.OS` for platform-specific behavior.
- Never write platform-specific logic inside component render — extract it.
- Test on both iOS and Android before marking any feature complete.

## Offline & Networking
- Every network request must handle loading, success, and error states.
- Implement optimistic updates for user-facing mutations.
- Use react-query / TanStack Query for all server state — not `useEffect` + `useState`.
- Cache responses aggressively. Define staleTime and cacheTime explicitly.
- Show offline indicators when the device loses connectivity.

## Storage
- Use `expo-secure-store` for sensitive data (tokens, keys). Never AsyncStorage for secrets.
- Encrypt any locally-persisted sensitive user data.
- Namespace all AsyncStorage / MMKV keys: `@appname/feature/key`.


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 3. AI / LLM INTEGRATION
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## AI Architecture
- Isolate ALL AI logic in `src/ai/`. No AI API calls scattered across components.
- Create an AI service layer — components should never call LLM APIs directly.
- Define a clear interface for AI responses — parse and validate before use.
- Maintain a single AI client instance (singleton pattern) to manage keys/config.

## API Keys & Security
- NEVER hardcode API keys. Use environment variables via `expo-constants` or `.env`.
- NEVER put API keys in client-side mobile code that ships to users.
  → Route all AI calls through your own backend proxy.
  → The backend holds the key; the client holds a session token.
- Rotate API keys if they are ever exposed in git history.
- Use `.gitignore` to exclude all `.env` files from version control.

## Prompt Engineering
- Store all prompt templates in `src/ai/prompts/` as named exports — not inline strings.
- Version your system prompts (e.g., `SYSTEM_PROMPT_V2`).
- Structure prompts with clear sections: context, instruction, constraints, output format.
- Always define the expected output format in the prompt (JSON schema, etc.).
- Validate AI output against the expected schema — never trust raw LLM output.
  ```ts
  // Example pattern
  const ResponseSchema = z.object({
    summary: z.string().max(500),
    tags: z.array(z.string()).max(10),
    confidence: z.number().min(0).max(1),
  });
  
  const parsed = ResponseSchema.safeParse(rawAIResponse);
  if (!parsed.success) handleAIParseError(parsed.error);
  ```

## Streaming & UX
- Implement streaming responses for any AI output longer than a sentence.
- Show a skeleton/typing indicator immediately when an AI request starts.
- Allow users to cancel in-flight AI requests (AbortController).
- Display partial streamed output as it arrives — don't wait for completion.
- For voice/audio AI: show waveform animation during processing.

## Context & Memory
- Track token usage per request — log it in development.
- Implement context window management: truncate or summarize old messages.
- Never send personally identifiable information (PII) to third-party AI APIs without explicit user consent and disclosure in your privacy policy.
- Strip or anonymize sensitive data before including it in prompts.
- Implement conversation history in a dedicated store, not component state.

## Error Handling for AI
- Handle these error classes explicitly:
  - Rate limit / quota exceeded (429) — implement exponential backoff with jitter.
  - Context length exceeded — auto-truncate and retry.
  - Content policy refusal — show a user-friendly message, log the prompt for review.
  - Network timeout — retry with backoff, max 3 attempts.
  - Malformed/unexpected response — fallback gracefully, never crash.
- Never surface raw API error messages to end users.

## AI Feature Flags
- Gate AI features behind feature flags for gradual rollout.
- Provide non-AI fallbacks for all AI-powered features where feasible.
- Track AI feature usage metrics separately from core app metrics.

## Model Selection
- Default to the most capable model available within your cost/latency budget.
- Document which model version is used for each feature — model behavior differs.
- Prefer newer model versions in new features, but pin versions in production.
  ```ts
  // Constants file
  export const AI_MODELS = {
    CHAT: 'claude-sonnet-4-20250514',      // Balanced: quality + cost
    FAST: 'claude-haiku-4-5-20251001',      // Low latency tasks
    VISION: 'claude-sonnet-4-20250514',    // Image analysis
  } as const;
  ```


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 4. STATE MANAGEMENT
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- Use Zustand for global client state (simple, low-boilerplate).
- Use TanStack Query for ALL server/async state — no exceptions.
- Component local state (`useState`) for UI-only state (modals, toggles).
- Never store derived state — compute it from source of truth.
- AI conversation state lives in a dedicated Zustand slice.
  ```ts
  // Slice pattern
  interface AIConversationSlice {
    messages: Message[];
    isStreaming: boolean;
    currentRequestId: string | null;
    addMessage: (msg: Message) => void;
    clearConversation: () => void;
    cancelStream: () => void;
  }
  ```


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 5. SECURITY & PRIVACY
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## Authentication
- Implement biometric authentication (Face ID / Fingerprint) for sensitive actions.
- Use short-lived access tokens + long-lived refresh tokens.
- Store tokens in `expo-secure-store` only.
- Implement automatic session expiry and re-authentication prompts.
- Log out and clear all local data on token invalidation.

## Data Handling
- Collect the minimum data required (data minimization principle).
- Never log user PII in production — scrub logs before shipping.
- Implement certificate pinning for production API calls.
- Validate and sanitize all user inputs before processing or sending.
- Implement HTTPS-only. No HTTP endpoints, ever.

## AI-Specific Privacy
- Document all data sent to AI providers in your privacy policy.
- Provide an in-app toggle to disable AI features (for privacy-conscious users).
- Implement data retention policies — don't store AI conversations longer than necessary.
- Never train or fine-tune models on user data without explicit opt-in consent.


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 6. ERROR HANDLING & RESILIENCE
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- Use Error Boundaries around every major screen section.
- Implement Sentry (or equivalent) for crash and error reporting from day one.
- All async functions must be wrapped in try/catch — no unhandled promise rejections.
- Define a global error handling utility:
  ```ts
  // utils/error-handler.ts
  export function handleError(error: unknown, context: string): void {
    const message = error instanceof Error ? error.message : 'Unknown error';
    logger.error(`[${context}] ${message}`, { error });
    // Report to Sentry in production
    if (!__DEV__) Sentry.captureException(error, { extra: { context } });
  }
  ```
- Show user-friendly error messages — never raw stack traces to users.
- Implement retry logic for transient failures (network, AI service).


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 7. ACCESSIBILITY (A11Y)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- Target WCAG 2.1 AA compliance as the baseline.
- All interactive elements need `accessibilityLabel`, `accessibilityHint`, `accessibilityRole`.
- Support Dynamic Type / font scaling — test at largest and smallest sizes.
- Color contrast ratio: minimum 4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text.
- Never convey information through color alone — add icons or text labels.
- Test with VoiceOver (iOS) and TalkBack (Android) before each release.
- AI-generated content must include an "AI-generated" accessibility label where relevant.


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 8. TESTING
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## Required Test Coverage
- Unit tests for all utility functions and hooks (Jest).
- Integration tests for all AI service functions — mock the LLM API.
- Component tests for all common components (React Native Testing Library).
- E2E tests for critical user flows: onboarding, core feature, AI interaction (Detox/Maestro).

## AI Testing
- Mock all LLM API calls in tests — never make real API calls in CI.
- Test with a fixed set of AI response fixtures covering happy path, errors, and edge cases.
- Test context management logic with boundary cases (empty, max length, truncation).
- Validate that prompt injection attempts are handled gracefully.
  ```ts
  // Example AI mock
  jest.mock('@/ai/client', () => ({
    complete: jest.fn().mockResolvedValue(mockAIResponse),
    stream: jest.fn().mockImplementation(mockStreamResponse),
  }));
  ```

## Test Naming
- Describe blocks use the component/function name.
- Test names follow: "should [expected behavior] when [condition]"
  ```ts
  describe('useAIChat', () => {
    it('should stream response tokens when message is sent', async () => { ... });
    it('should handle rate limit error with exponential backoff', async () => { ... });
    it('should clear conversation when clearConversation is called', () => { ... });
  });
  ```


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 9. PERFORMANCE BENCHMARKS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- App cold start < 2 seconds on a mid-range device.
- Screen transitions < 300ms.
- AI first-token response < 1 second (with streaming).
- FlatList scroll: maintain 60 FPS (monitor with Flashlight or Perfetto).
- Bundle size: monitor with `expo-bundle-analyzer`. Keep initial bundle < 5MB.
- Use `React.Profiler` in development to catch unnecessary re-renders.
- All images must be optimized: WebP format, appropriate resolution per screen density.


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 10. GIT & COLLABORATION
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## Commit Messages (Conventional Commits)
```
<type>(<scope>): <short summary>

Types: feat | fix | refactor | perf | test | docs | chore | style
Scope: ai | auth | ui | navigation | storage | notifications

Examples:
feat(ai): add streaming support for chat responses
fix(auth): resolve token refresh race condition
perf(ui): memoize MessageList item to reduce re-renders
```

## Branch Strategy
- `main` — production-ready, always deployable
- `develop` — integration branch
- `feature/description` — new features
- `fix/description` — bug fixes
- `ai/description` — AI feature work (separate to enable easy review)

## PR Requirements
- No PR without tests for new logic.
- AI-related PRs must include a sample prompt and response in the PR description.
- Security-sensitive PRs (auth, storage, AI keys) require 2 reviewers.
- Never approve a PR with hardcoded credentials, even test ones.


